  "eraEmissionSchedule": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "The era emission schedule. Rewards here are a function of block number and, for an uncle, of how far back it was mined -- no state, no transaction -- so these are computable and checkable without executing a block. THE ACTIVATION IS A NON-EVENT, and that is the first thing to assert: the rule takes effect at block 5,000,000 but that block falls in era 0, whose reward is the unchanged 5e18. Nothing moves until 5,000,001. A fixture testing the activation height alone would pass against a client that had not implemented the rule at all. THE UNCLE REWARD IS THE LARGE CHANGE, not the block reward. Through era 0 an uncle miner is paid on how recent the uncle is, (8 - distance)/8 of the full reward, which at distance one is 4.375e18. From era 1 that is replaced by a flat thirty-second of the era reward, 1.25e17 -- a drop of thirty-five times, against the block reward's fall of a fifth. The distance table is carried for every vector so the change from distance-sensitive to flat is visible as a shape and not just as a number: through era 0 the six entries differ from each other, and from era 1 they are identical. The includer's own per-uncle reward is a thirty-second in every era, so it moves only with the era reward and never with the formula.",
      "oracle": "Computed independently from ECIP-1017 -- era length 5,000,000, a four-fifths disinflation per era, and the two uncle formulas -- then cross-checked against the production client's own reward vectors, which agree on all eleven block numbers they cover.",
      "scope": "These are emission values, not block-level state transitions. Asserting that a mined block credits these amounts still needs a block-level runner, which the transition tool cannot supply: its reward flag is a single flat value applied uniformly and never consults the chain configuration's era schedule.",
      "generated": "2026-08-23"
    },
